 # Depth from Ray Distance
-
-
 ## Overview
 Depth from Ray Distance converts the scalar ray distance $d$ (the Euclidean distance from the camera center to the intersection or accumulation point along the ray) into camera-space depth $z_c$ (the distance along the camera's optical axis) [S1]. It solves the problem of interpreting differentiable volume rendering outputs, which naturally produce distances along the viewing ray, into a coordinate system compatible with standard depth maps and RGB-D sensors [S2]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Expected Term Rendering](5952-expected-term-rendering.md) and [Median Depth Rendering](8815-median-depth-rendering.md) by providing a direct geometric projection of the ray distance rather than computing statistical moments or threshold crossings of the volume density [G].
