 ### RGB-D Mapping Optimization
-Photometric loss $L_p$ is the mean squared error between rendered color $\hat{C}$ and input color $C$: $L_p(\hat{C}(\sigma, c)) = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{M} ||\hat{C}(\sigma_i, c_i) - C||^2$ [S2]. Geometric depth loss $L_g$ is the mean squared error between rendered depth $\hat{D}$ and input depth $D$: $L_g(\hat{D}(\sigma)) = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{i=1}^{M} ||\hat{D}(\sigma) - D||^2$ [S2]. Total loss is $L = L_p + \lambda_d L_g$, where $\lambda_d$ is the scaling factor of the geometric loss [S2]. Partial derivative of rendered color $\hat{C}$ w.r.t. color $c_i$ is $\frac{\partial \hat{C}}{\partial c_i} = w_i(\sigma) = T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i))$ [S2]. Partial derivative of rendered depth $\hat{D}$ w.r.t. density $\sigma_i$ is $\frac{\partial \hat{D}}{\partial \sigma_i} = \delta_i [ t_i T_{i+1}(\sigma) - \hat{D} + \sum_{j=0}^{i} t_j w_j(\sigma) ]$, derived by interpreting distance $t_i$ as color $c_i$ [S2]. The derivatives are implemented directly in a custom CUDA kernel, building on the code developed for Plenoxel to boost computational speed [S2].
